<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Population transfer in the Soviet Union</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">From 1930 to 1952 the government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> and under the direction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Lavrentiy Beria</a>, forcibly transferred whole groups of people, mostly to remote and underpopulated areas. Those deported included &quot;anti-Soviet&quot; categories, entire ethnic groups such as the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> in 1937, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a> and Ingush and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>, and labour forces. At least 6 million people were affected, among them 1.8 million kulaks in 1930–31. Soviet archives record hundreds of thousands of deaths, and Nicolas Werth places the total near 1 to 1.5 million. In 1991 a law of the Russian republic denounced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> as a policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The transfers fell into four broad kinds: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> of categories labelled anti-Soviet, deportations of whole ethnic groups, transfers of labour, and organised migrations in the opposite direction to fill emptied territories. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dekulakization">Dekulakization</a> was the first time an entire class was deported. Many farmers who resisted collectivization were labelled kulaks whatever their income or property, and kulak resettlement continued until early 1950, with one of the last orders, on 5 September 1951, concerning the Lithuanian SSR.</p><p>During the 1930s the category of enemies of the people shifted from class terms to ethnic ones, and between 1935 and 1938 at least ten nationalities were deported. The removal of almost the whole Soviet Korean population, 171,781 people, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-far-east">Russian Far East</a> to the Kazakh SSR and the Uzbek SSR in October 1937 was the first mass transfer of an entire nationality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After the Soviet annexation of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1939, 1.45 million people of the region were deported between 1939 and 1941. Polish historians put the share of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> at 63.1 per cent and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> at 7.4 per cent, and estimate that about 350,000 of those deported from 1939 to 1945 died. In 1943 and 1944 some 1.9 million people were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> and the Central Asian republics, among them Karachays, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmyks">Kalmyks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>, Ingush, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkars">Balkars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a>.</p><p>After the war the Germans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast">Kaliningrad Oblast</a> were expelled and the area was resettled mainly by Russians. Between 1944 and 1953 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and others were moved away from border regions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Soviet archives record 390,000 deaths during kulak resettlement and up to 400,000 among those deported to forced settlements in the 1940s. Nicolas Werth places the total nearer 1 to 1.5 million. Recorded mortality among the deported ranged from 14.6 per cent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskhetian-turks">Meskhetian Turks</a> to 23.7 per cent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a>, Ingush and other North Caucasian peoples, caused by climate, disease, malnutrition and heavy labour.</p><p>In February 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a> condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> of whole nations as violations of Leninist principles, and decrees that year lifted restrictions on the deported peoples, without a right of return to their homelands. The Supreme Soviet declared the acts illegal and criminal on 14 November 1989, and a law of 26 April 1991 denounced them as a policy of slander and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Historians such as Pavel Polian call them crimes against humanity. The European Parliament recognised the deportation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chechens">Chechens</a> and Ingush as genocide in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Population transfer in the Soviet Union” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
